17592186510347 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186510228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510347 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510347 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510347 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186509696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510347 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d9278757-a57e-52ac-9515-43ca861ad9fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510376 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186510296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510376 #edb/ref 1069 "q-70492" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510376 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510376 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the sphere ${x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2}+(z-2)^{2}=9$ and the line $x=y+1=\\frac{z-2}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510376 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2fc9ea0f-3211-5796-852b-f82127f7f8bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510376 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write the equation of our line in parametric form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{1}=\\frac{y+1}{1}=\\frac{z-2}{2}=t\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t \\\\ y=t-1 \\\\ z=2t+2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ into the equation of the sphere, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2}+(z-2)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (t)^{2}+((t-1)+4)^{2}+((2t+2)-2)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (t)^{2}+(t+3)^{2}+(2t)^{2} & =9 \\\\ {t}^{2}+({t}^{2}+6t+9)+4{t}^{2} & =9 \\\\ 6{t}^{2}+6t+9 & =9 \\\\ 6{t}^{2}+6t & =0 \\\\ t(t+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=0$ and $t=-1.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=(0)=0 \\\\ y=(0)-1=-1 \\\\ z=2(0)+2=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(0,-1,2).$\n\n- For $t=-1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=(-1)=-1 \\\\ y=(-1)-1=-2 \\\\ z=2(-1)+2=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(-1,-2,0).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(0,-1,2)$ and $(-1,-2,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510389 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186510347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510389 #edb/ref 1069 "q-35493" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510389 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510389 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the projection of the intersection between the hyperboloid of two sheets \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{y}^{2}-\\frac{(z+2)^{2}}{3}=-1$\nand the plane $3y+z=-2$  onto the\n$xy$-plane." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510389 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4aa33ace-7f3a-5239-9095-80fa2dcc03c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510389 #edb/ref 1074 "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xy$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $z.$ First, we solve the equation of the plane for $z$, as follows:\n\n$$\n3y+z=-2\\,⟹\\,z=-2-3y\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $z$ into the equation of the hyperboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{y}^{2}-\\frac{((-2-3y)+2)^{2}}{3} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{y}^{2}-\\frac{9{y}^{2}}{3} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-2{y}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4{y}^{2} & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the hyperbola ${x}^{2}-4{y}^{2}=-2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510411 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186510283 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510411 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71144" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510411 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510411 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the projection of the intersection between the ellipsoid $\\frac{2(x-4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{3}=1$ and the plane $2x-z=8$ onto the $xy$-plane." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510411 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "79a93a5e-93ce-572a-a9b2-6cc224e62f04" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510411 #edb/ref 1074 "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xy$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $z.$ First, we solve the equation of the plane for $z$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-z & =8\\,⟹\\,z=2x-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $z$ into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2(x-4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(2x-8)^{2}}{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{2(x-4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(2(x-4))^{2}}{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{2(x-4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{4(x-4)^{2}}{3} & =1 \\\\ 2(x-4)^{2}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{1/2}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{1/2}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510412 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186510253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510412 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94778" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510412 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510412 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the points of the intersection of the elliptic cone ${x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}=4{z}^{2}$ and the line $\\frac{x+1}{2}=y=z+2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510412 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "79e25129-bb03-5263-b94d-378f30c636ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510412 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write the equation of our line in the parametric form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+1}{2}=y=z+2=t\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2t-1 \\\\ y=t \\\\ z=t-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2} & =4{z}^{2} \\\\ (2t-1)^{2}+3{t}^{2} & =4(t-2)^{2} \\\\ 4{t}^{2}-4t+1+3{t}^{2} & =4({t}^{2}-4t+4) \\\\ 4{t}^{2}-4t+1+3{t}^{2} & =4{t}^{2}-16t+16 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}+12t-15 & =0 \\\\ 3({t}^{2}+4t-5) & =0 \\\\ 3(t+5)(t-1) & =0 \\\\ t & =-5,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2(-5)-1=-11 \\\\ y=-5 \\\\ z=-5-2=-7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(-11,-5,-7).$\n\n- For $t=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2(1)-1=1 \\\\ y=1 \\\\ z=1-2=-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(1,1,-1).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-11,-5,-7)$ and $(1,1,-1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510479 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186510523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510479 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "07630e9e-6eaa-5c9c-8cca-3f82529d648d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510479 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186510411 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510523 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8cafab22-633e-567a-b060-34f181932e1e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510523 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186510412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510542 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186510479 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510542 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cb05386a-05eb-5248-b754-a8d8d5a583d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510542 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186510376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510550 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186510565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510550 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e330baa0-4535-5227-ae2f-5e7087691796" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510550 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186510389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510565 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186510542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510565 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f9fa29ad-e929-5ec2-a636-229382ae0cca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510565 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186496282 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
